WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.710
- So just as a tiny bit of introduction,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.870
mentorship can be defined
in a lot of different ways,

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.600
but the way I think about it anyway,

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.180
is that it encompasses a lot of things.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.200
Mentors can be your advisors or PIs

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:28.290
who are formally responsible
for your academic progress,

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.330
it can be supporters of your personal

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:35.050
and professional endeavors,
teachers, even trainers,

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.473
maybe you have sponsors and allies,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.070
maybe you have academic role models

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.770
as future faculty and
leaders beyond the academy,

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.550
really anyone who you
can find who can help you

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.433
reach your professional goals.

00:00:50.252 --> 00:00:54.190
So on the flip side, we
expect you, our students,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:57.960
to weave together the network you need

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.870
of resources and advisors,
so that you can pursue

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.800
your intellectual and professional goals.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.210
And your needs from mentors
will vary of course,

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.820
across your disciplines
and the type of degree

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.290
you're working on, as well as
where you are in your program.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.150
What you need as a beginning student

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.903
will change over time
as you make progress.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.970
However, and there are some things,

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.080
some shared values and
goals and responsibilities

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.750
that really underpin all effective
mentorship relationships,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.150
and those include the
need to be self-directed,

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.920
to be really intrinsically motivated,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.760
and to be able to persevere
through rough spots,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.113
'cause we all have those.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.200
And if you can do that and reach out,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.910
you can definitely find
supportive scholars

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:49.390
and friends to make
your graduate education

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.080
wonderful in a collaborative way.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.830
You are definitely not alone,

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:58.120
Yale is teaming with advisors and mentors,

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.880
and that includes faculty of course,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.760
but also deans, and staff,
advanced students, peers,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.410
really, this is a people
to people endeavor

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.050
we're doing and you should tap into

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.300
all the help you can get.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.870
So today we're focusing on a particular

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.350
type of mentorship and
that's exemplified in

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:26.100
the grad school by our annual
graduate mentor awards,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.360
and these celebrate faculty members

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.970
who have been outstanding
teachers and advisors

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.060
of graduate students, and
the three faculty members

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.150
who are here today on the
panel are all past winners

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.913
of the graduate mentor award,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.310
which means they were
nominated by their current,

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.620
or past, or both, mentees for this award,

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.870
for their exemplary behaviors.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.210
So they really exemplify
the important qualities

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.670
to which all graduate teachers

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.760
and faculty mentors should aspire.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.290
So just brief introductions.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:08.290
Gary Brudvig is the Silliman
Professor of Chemistry,

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.090
and Professor Brudvig's students

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.580
have remarked that somehow he manages

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.770
to, sorry, I lost my place,

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.223
I'm moving my screen around,

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.910
somehow he manages to be inspiring

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:26.230
and scientifically
challenging at the same time

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.873
without being intimidating or demanding.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.640
He allows his mentees to use
their time in graduate school

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.300
to develop as scientists and people

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.123
in the ways we find most valuable.

00:03:39.247 --> 00:03:41.847
One student wrote, quote, this is a quote,

00:03:41.847 --> 00:03:44.050
"Of course, the chemistry
is always the main goal,

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.860
but Gary also encourages
students to spend time

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.870
taking extra classes,
gaining teaching skills,

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.690
and leading outreach efforts.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.380
I think the best way to
sum up what makes Gary

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.470
such a great advisor and
mentor is that he allows

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.660
for each of his students
to have the flexibility

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:00.960
to follow their own passions."

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.870
So Gary thanks for being here.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.240
- Yeah, my pleasure.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.630
- Next, Kelly Shue is Professor of Finance

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.580
at the School of Management.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:12.650
Professor Shue is known
to go above and beyond

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.793
to support her PhD advisees.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.000
Students note that she cares deeply

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.230
about their personal
and professional success

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.090
and does everything she can to help them

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:24.600
reach their full potential as scholars.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.960
One student remarked, "In a year when

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.300
many academic conferences
have been canceled

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.400
or gone virtual, Professor
Shue tried to make up

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.410
for the lost networking opportunities

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.300
by connecting students
with other faculty members

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.020
who do work in the same fields,

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.840
she will go the extra
mile for her advisees."

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.963
Thanks for being here and
thanks for all of that.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.120
And our third faculty
member here with us today

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.020
is Joanne Meyerowitz, who is the Arthur

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.280
Unobskey Professor of History

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.630
and Professor of American Studies.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.030
Professor Meyerowitz' students esteem her

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.800
as a dream advisor who teaches students

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.300
to read history while
sharpening their abilities

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.940
to identify trends in
historical scholarship.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.347
One nominator wrote that she is

00:05:11.347 --> 00:05:13.710
"Timely and thoughtful in her engagement

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.550
with student work, frank about the rewards

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:19.230
and difficulties of the
academic job market,

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.030
and consistently helpful
in directing her students

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:24.940
toward resources and
opportunities to develop

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.090
their scholarship and
intellectual community."

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:31.910
So thank you, all three of
you, as exceptional mentors

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:35.080
for providing insights today to students

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:36.783
and anyone else who's tuned in.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.850
We are lucky to have you
here, thank you for doing it.

00:05:43.328 --> 00:05:45.980
So now what I'd like to
do is hand the floor over

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:49.040
to Assistant Dean for
Graduate Student Life,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.570
Matthew Tanico, who will
moderate the discussion.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.253
So please enjoy everyone.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:56.070
- Thank you, Lynn.

00:05:57.095 --> 00:05:58.760
I just wanna reiterate
Dean Cooley's gratitude

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.840
for our panelist
participation this afternoon.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.690
So we'll start with a few questions

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.780
that we've prepared in
advance for our panelists

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.360
and we'll reserve the
latter part of the panel

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.140
for Q and A from the audience,

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.340
so feel free to put questions in the chat,

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.690
I'll do my best to include questions

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:17.460
as they come up in the discussion,

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.230
but you can also save
questions till the end

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:22.543
and use the raise hand
feature to ask those live.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.670
So I wanted to say that the word mentor

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:28.840
takes its etymological origin

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.070
from the character Mentor in the Odyssey.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.570
And Mentor was the teacher and advisor

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:36.280
to Odysseus's son Telemachus

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.110
after Odysseus left for the Trojan War.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:43.460
So that's to say that mentorship
is an ancient practice,

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.860
but to be effective, it also must account

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.830
for contemporary issues.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.630
And so I hope that today's
discussion will address

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:54.270
the current needs of
both mentors and mentees

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.400
within higher education to build

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.460
the kinds of effective
mentorship relationships

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:00.403
that Lynn was describing earlier.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.360
So I wanna start off the
conversation by asking

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.590
each of our faculty
panelists to talk about

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:09.030
their personal mentoring philosophies

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.100
and how they've adapted their practices

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.650
to the current realities
of graduate education,

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:15.930
which is both the
realities of the pandemic,

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.270
but also the changing nature

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.900
of what graduate education is for.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:21.590
So I open the floor up to whomever

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:24.373
wants to begin with the discussion.

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:33.310
- Well, okay, I'll just jump right in.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:35.500
So I would say that basically,

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:38.840
I see my role as an
advocate and a supporter

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:42.743
of the students who I advise
and who are in my classes,

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:46.200
but as an advocate who's willing to push,

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.920
who's willing to suggest ways to navigate

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.690
the institution and
especially to suggest ways

00:07:54.672 --> 00:07:58.180
to improve scholarship, I believe in

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:01.020
sort of giving honest, and kind,

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.700
constructive feedback, but again,

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:05.300
let me emphasize the honest part,

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.700
I think it's really important to be honest

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.570
at the same time as kind and constructive.

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:13.610
And I'd say that my aim
is to work with students

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:16.220
to accomplish what they wanna accomplish,

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.480
to help them accomplish
what they wanna accomplish,

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:20.870
and that means listening
to what they want,

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:24.450
which isn't necessarily what I think

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:27.430
should be the topic of their
scholarship or whatever,

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:30.160
that's really up to the
students I work with,

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:32.943
and my goal is to help
them reach their goals.

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:36.910
And sometimes occasionally
that means intervening,

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:39.610
being a mediator on their behalf,

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:42.740
say intervening with
other faculty members,

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:46.190
or back when I was at DGS,
intervening with deans,

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:49.560
but only if a student requests
that kind of mediation,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.950
that seems really
important to me to respect

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.590
a certain kind of, within the law,

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.370
certain kind of confidentiality.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.670
And you asked about change over time too,

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:02.320
about sort of the recent changes,

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.580
and I would say that more recently

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.380
I've been giving more attention
to nonacademic careers,

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.800
as we all have, and the
possibilities for that

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.230
and listening to what it is that students

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:16.090
want to do with their
lives, and that could mean

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:18.370
following a non-academic trajectory

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:21.060
after getting a PhD, it
could mean dropping out

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.840
before getting a PhD,
I have students who've

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:27.900
chosen to forgo the dissertation and go be

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.890
a labor organizer or go
be a high school teacher,

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:34.310
and sometimes they think
that they're disappointing me

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:37.680
in doing that, and I wanna reassure them

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:39.490
that it's not a disappointment,

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.550
I want them to flourish, I want them to do

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.840
what they wanna do, and
I wanna support them

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.763
in whatever that is, and
whatever they've learned at Yale,

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.550
that will shape, it will
inform their lives after Yale,

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:52.923
whatever the career is.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.030
And finally, I would say
that I pay more attention

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.070
now to mental health issues.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.320
I think that we're all becoming more aware

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.113
of just how far graduate school can be.

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:10.223
- I'm happy to follow up.

00:10:12.007 --> 00:10:16.590
So I try to actually follow

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.410
and get a read on what
the student would like

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.593
in terms of a mentoring relationship.

00:10:22.792 --> 00:10:26.010
And I think graduate students
are highly idiosyncratic,

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:28.550
some people want a really
high touch relationship

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:31.670
in which I check in on them regularly,

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.540
they touch in with me regularly,

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.720
I think that can be a very effective,

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:38.370
other people can do very well with more

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.917
kind of formal meetings
or check in via email,

00:10:41.917 --> 00:10:44.340
and if that's their
preferred mode of contact,

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:46.660
I think that can still work.

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:48.750
I think the benefit of
a university environment

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:51.090
is we can handle all
kinds of personalities

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:54.450
and preferences for
mentoring relationships

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.083
and also research styles.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:01.890
So I'm a Professor in the
Finance group at Yale SOM,

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:04.480
and within the research
disciplines of finance,

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.370
economics, et cetera, we
don't really run labs,

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.280
it's really, there's students

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:13.550
who are doing their
independent research projects,

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.430
they might sometimes
coauthor with faculty,

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:18.750
but they're not tied to any particular

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:22.230
faculty member through a
laboratory relationship.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:24.470
I think because of that, I try very hard

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:28.010
not to be territorial over students,

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:30.390
in fact, I really encourage them to find

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:34.510
multiple mentors, partly
so that they can get

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:37.780
multiple points of view,
but it also kind of

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.350
diversifies their risk
in terms of what if one

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:43.770
faculty member moves or gives advice

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:47.350
that ends up not being useful,

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:50.410
or let's say student
wants to work on something

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.730
interdisciplinary, so
they really need to make

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:55.923
a committee of people with
multiple areas of expertise.

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:01.050
Matthew had also asked about adapting to

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:05.260
modern demands, especially
with the pandemic.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:09.260
What I've tried to do is I offer students

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.180
to meet over Zoom or in my office,

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:13.130
some people actually
prefer the convenience

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.850
of meeting over Zoom, doesn't require

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:17.380
that they're on campus,
especially if they have

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:20.683
family responsibilities or
they're stuck in another country.

00:12:22.374 --> 00:12:26.640
And I've tried to be more of a listener

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:30.740
in terms of their other
problems in their lives,

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:34.260
it's important, I think that
I don't probe too much or ask,

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:37.762
but a lot of these problems do
just come up in conversation,

00:12:37.762 --> 00:12:39.140
I think it's very useful
actually for me to know

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:42.260
just the context of
these research problems,

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:45.190
but then that's in a
context of a whole set of

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:47.383
issues that my students are dealing with.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.140
- Yeah, I guess I can follow suit.

00:12:53.014 --> 00:12:54.580
So I'm in the Chemistry Department

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:56.890
and we work in a lab as a team,

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:00.995
so I'll talk more in
terms of the mentoring

00:13:00.995 --> 00:13:02.823
in that kind of an environment.

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:07.660
Graduate school is a
transition point where

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:10.320
you go from being an undergraduate
where you take courses,

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.730
and you learn, and
reiterate things that are

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:17.110
in the courses, to independent research

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:18.910
where you're blazing your own trail,

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:22.690
and you're doing independent work,

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:24.910
and you're not following
directions anymore,

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:28.020
and so my goal is to facilitate that,

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:31.070
and as Joanne said, to achieve your goals

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:33.800
in the end, and those goals might be

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:35.710
to become an academic or it may be

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:39.380
to work in industry, maybe
to become a consultant

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:41.660
or lots of different
directions, go to law school,

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:45.003
those are all directions students
from my group have taken.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:50.020
And yeah, so within a team environment,

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:53.270
so one of the things I
really work to do is to

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:57.403
encourage team efforts and
people working together,

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:01.800
and that creates a level of interaction

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:05.270
and engagement that I think leads to

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:08.870
less isolation in a team effort.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.600
You're not on your own, you're
really working as a team,

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:13.860
but on the other hand, everyone has to

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.873
follow their own path
and become independent

00:14:16.873 --> 00:14:18.123
and learn how to do that.

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:23.670
And that's my goal, and
encouraging independent ideas.

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:27.420
I'm pretty much a hands off advisor,

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:29.930
and so I'm not looking every day, okay,

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.940
do this next, do that next, but really

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:36.870
we talk as a group every
week, many times a week,

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:40.460
actually, and it's to brainstorm together,

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:44.450
and work towards a
successful path forward.

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:46.590
And so that's my idea of mentorship,

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:51.590
and I think the pandemic has certainly

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:55.410
made it more difficult
for in person engagement,

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:58.240
but on the other hand,
it's also by using Zoom

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:02.023
as an opportunity to have
frequent face to face connections.

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:06.620
One of my challenges is my
research group is split,

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:08.580
part of my group is in
the Chemistry Department

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:10.410
on main campus, and the rest of my group

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:13.320
is on the west campus on the
Energy Science Institute.

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:15.920
And so it's not always
so easy to see each other

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:18.720
face to face all the time,
because back and forth travel,

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:20.400
but Zoom actually makes that a lot easier,

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:23.310
so there are some benefits to

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:25.760
that transition to
Zoom, we'll probably not

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:30.093
stop doing that, even when
things get more back to normal.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:33.690
- Thank you all, that's great.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:36.050
I think there's a clear sense of having

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:38.610
a student centered approach
and giving the students

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:40.610
independence while either connecting them

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:43.330
with resources or enabling them to pursue

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:45.160
those resources on their own,
that really comes through

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:46.310
in all of your answers.

00:15:48.722 --> 00:15:52.040
There is currently a
trend in student affairs

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.100
to talk about the whole person approach,

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:57.790
and that it's impossible
to completely separate out

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:00.100
our personal and professional lives,

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:03.700
and yet boundaries are
necessary to maintain

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:07.010
healthy interpersonal
interactions across the board.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.910
So could you talk a bit
about how you maintain

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:11.900
healthy relationships with your mentees

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:13.990
and what advice you would give students

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:16.840
for setting and respecting
boundaries with their mentors?

00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:25.360
- I guess, I don't know if we wanna follow

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:27.540
the same sequence every
time, but I'll go this way,

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:31.380
we'll break the trend and we
can mix it up a little bit,

00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:32.990
but I was gonna mention this before,

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:36.140
but please ask questions,

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:37.870
I'm more than happy to be interrupted,

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:40.470
and if you have any
questions you wanna follow up

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:43.700
or just ask something, please do so,

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:46.760
I think Matt's looking at the chat

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:49.723
if you wanna add something to the chat.

00:16:51.729 --> 00:16:55.690
So as I mentioned, in a science group,

00:16:55.690 --> 00:16:59.810
we work as a team, and most of the time

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:02.510
that is quite professional,
we're working on research,

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:06.030
talking about new results,
new plans for experiments,

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:09.230
and so on, but it also
creates kind of a family,

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:12.230
and we do things together, and so

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:15.050
it's not a complete division between

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:18.000
the science that we do and other things

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.240
outside of the lab as well,

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:22.490
so I think that's something that

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:26.633
is a good aspect of a
team that works in a lab,

00:17:28.138 --> 00:17:30.823
and for example, my own research group,

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:33.700
they organized game nights during,

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:36.217
when we were shut down with COVID,

00:17:36.217 --> 00:17:37.940
and so we did that on Zoom,

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:41.170
and that was fun, and not just

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:43.330
actually about a month ago,
we had a group ski trip,

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:46.250
and so we do some of those kind of things

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:49.173
just to foster a team effort,

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:53.830
but then I think everybody
has their own personal life

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:57.300
and that's separate, so
separate from the group,

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:58.980
and that's an important boundary

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:02.063
that I think I respect.

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:08.210
- Yeah, I would agree
that there are definitely

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:10.700
boundaries to respect,
I think that when I was,

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:12.730
I mean, I work more
one on one with people,

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:15.220
not in a lab setting,
so it's not that sort of

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:19.440
same group team building
spirit, except in the classroom.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.240
And I think that when I was younger,

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:23.670
I felt, when I was much younger,

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:26.100
I felt more like a friend or a peer

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.560
with some of my students,
with my graduate students,

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:31.010
some of whom were
actually older than I was

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:33.210
way back then, but now I'm older

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:37.500
and I feel friendly,
but not like a friend,

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:40.763
and so I feel it's more of
a professional relationship.

00:18:42.620 --> 00:18:47.620
I don't expect to be part of
my student's personal lives,

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:50.490
unless I'm explicitly invited to be,

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:53.170
invited to meet a partner or parents,

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:56.720
or go to a wedding or something like that,

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:59.320
but mostly professional, and I would say

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:01.970
I like to make myself available

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:04.253
to meet with advisees and students,

00:19:05.930 --> 00:19:08.010
and I'm always happy to go out for coffee

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:11.180
or have lunch, but usually
that's in a campus setting,

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:13.740
not always, but almost
always in a campus setting,

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:16.593
again, that sort of
professional feeling to it.

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:20.160
And I know that there can be a kind of

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:24.610
fine line between
mentoring and surveillance,

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:27.780
and I want to be careful
not to cross that line,

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.860
I don't want anybody to feel
like I'm monitoring them

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:34.200
or invading their lives, and so really

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:37.540
it's more of expecting
students to come to me

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:39.110
when they need something from me

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:41.840
or want something from
me, and to make clear that

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:44.950
I'm a presence there for
them when they need me,

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:47.100
but not a constant
presence in their lives.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:51.780
- Yeah, for me, personally,

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:54.810
this has so far not been much of an issue,

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:56.870
although I do have mixed views

00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:59.270
on this question in general.

00:19:59.270 --> 00:20:01.090
So personally I maintain a fairly

00:20:01.090 --> 00:20:04.290
professional relationship with my mentees.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:06.980
What I've noticed is
that after they graduate

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:09.430
we'll continue talking,
but then we move toward

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:12.260
a less of a professional
relationship and we can talk more

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:13.980
as colleagues and friends,

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:16.330
but that typically
happens after graduation

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.483
when my students get their first jobs.

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:22.600
I do have mixed views on this,

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:26.133
because I know there's
research showing that,

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:30.440
I think the research is about the unequal

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:33.050
access to schmoozing
and how much schmoozing

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:34.973
matters for career advancement.

00:20:36.513 --> 00:20:39.060
But I do worry that the knee jerk reaction

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.910
to this research is
then we're just going to

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:43.800
ban all channels for schmoozing,

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:46.690
so faculty are perhaps
not going to be allowed

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:49.630
to invite students to
their homes for parties,

00:20:49.630 --> 00:20:51.670
or we can't have meals with students,

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:54.590
because, well, if we can't offer the same

00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:56.320
opportunity to all students,

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:58.200
then we just shouldn't do it at all.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:01.190
And I'm not sure that's
the right way to go.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:04.060
Instead, it really has to be about

00:21:04.060 --> 00:21:07.180
students have to match
with the set of faculty

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:09.750
that they choose and they
have a good match with,

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:11.770
and part of that is through perhaps

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:14.730
more informal activities and talking about

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:16.830
more than just research.

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.603
So I'm not sure where
the right balance is,

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:22.590
but I would hope that we don't completely

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:25.923
ban or rule out these
opportunities as a school.

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:32.910
- So I think that's super helpful.

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:35.610
Building on that sort of how you approach

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:39.010
these relationships and build
out these relationships,

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:42.300
I think knowing what you want
and asking for what you need,

00:21:42.300 --> 00:21:45.090
and knowing the difference
between those two things

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:46.983
is an essential piece of it,

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:51.660
and so negotiation, clear communication,

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:54.740
these are the things that
are crucial to making

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:56.293
that relationship successful.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:21:59.160
So what advice would you
give to students for actually

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.580
communicating with their
mentors effectively,

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:05.330
and whether that's in
meetings, or via email,

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:09.980
or how often they should be
speaking with their mentors?

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:12.270
And I'm thinking also here more broadly

00:22:12.270 --> 00:22:15.900
than just the faculty advisor
to student relationship,

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:17.640
I think we're getting at something here,

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:20.070
which is that you're
building a sort of culture

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:22.290
around yourself in some
of these relationships

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:25.030
and figuring out who to ask for when.

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:27.650
And so do you all have any advice around

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:31.093
sort of how to do that effectively
with the communication?

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:43.880
- One thing that I tell
all my students to do is,

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:46.250
maybe this applies more for students

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:49.190
for which on their dissertations
is really their job

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:51.570
to come up with a completely
independent topic,

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.793
as is the case in finance and economics,

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:57.800
what I tell them is, when they ask faculty

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:00.030
for feedback on research ideas,

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:04.900
is to ask them really to rank three ideas,

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:08.500
or rank more than one idea, and the idea

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:12.033
behind this advice is we would
like to give honest feedback,

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:16.890
but we also want to be
nice and supportive people.

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:18.470
So oftentimes if a student just

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:22.280
comes to our office with one
idea, we're gonna try to get

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:24.140
as much out of that idea as possible,

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:28.130
even if that idea may face various hurdles

00:23:28.130 --> 00:23:31.150
to publication or job market success.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:35.210
So coming to our office
hours or coming to talk to us

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:37.940
with three possible avenues of research

00:23:39.037 --> 00:23:41.800
or specialization just allows us to

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:45.163
give more honest and helpful feedback.

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:53.270
- So I guess if my main piece of advice

00:23:53.270 --> 00:23:58.270
would be to stay in
touch, to don't disappear.

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:03.580
So I think that mentors
and faculty advisors

00:24:03.580 --> 00:24:05.880
and professors don't want to feel like

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:10.670
we have to chase after you
to find out how you're doing,

00:24:10.670 --> 00:24:13.690
so that may sometimes be
that I'll take the initiative

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:15.990
and get in touch with
people, but generally

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:18.860
I expect students I work with to

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.650
let me know how they're
doing, and I don't mean

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:24.230
on a weekly basis or
daily basis, I just mean

00:24:24.230 --> 00:24:26.310
make sure they staying in touch

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:28.630
and letting me know what's going on,

00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:31.100
not in their personal lives,
but in their professional lives

00:24:31.100 --> 00:24:34.160
and if need be in their
personal lives as well,

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:38.190
if they choose, so I think
that, that staying in touch is

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:43.930
not just about coming to
someone when you have a problem,

00:24:43.930 --> 00:24:46.880
although that's a really
important thing to do,

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:49.720
but I think also to share your triumphs

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:53.640
with your professors, to
what are you accomplishing?

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:54.603
Where are you?

00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:58.680
Your mentors wanna hear about
your hard spots you're having,

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:01.330
but your mentors will take great pride

00:25:01.330 --> 00:25:03.320
in your accomplishments,
I mean, I can't tell you,

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:06.580
I feel real joy when one of my students

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:09.070
has some great accomplishment
and I want them

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:12.610
to let me know, so I
guess it's a matter of

00:25:12.610 --> 00:25:16.210
maintaining a conversation across time,

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:19.740
and not, again, daily,
not necessarily weekly,

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:23.120
but just staying in touch, I
think that's really important

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:25.373
when there's a problem,
and even when there's not.

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:30.380
- Yeah, I guess it's a little
bit different in the sciences,

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:32.090
it's not that we're not
staying in touch, I mean,

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:34.080
I meet with everybody
in my group every week,

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:37.040
multiple times, but I'm not a mind reader,

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:40.490
so I don't know what
your problems might be

00:25:42.128 --> 00:25:43.800
and things that you'd like advice on,

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:46.157
you have to ask, and so I think

00:25:47.548 --> 00:25:50.220
setting up meetings and discussing

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.200
any topic you want, I mean, it could be

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:56.520
ranging from a different
research direction

00:25:56.520 --> 00:26:01.160
versus a career pathway, or any aspect

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:02.763
in your career development.

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:07.490
I can tell you things, but
it may not be what you're

00:26:07.490 --> 00:26:10.870
really concerned about or need advice on,

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:13.570
so I think asking, and in my own case,

00:26:13.570 --> 00:26:17.370
I have, well before COVID,
an open door policy now,

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:19.450
I guess an open contact policy,

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:23.460
whether it's a Zoom meeting
or an email or what,

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:27.060
and I think encouraged
that, as Joanne said,

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:30.620
you've got stay in touch, tell us

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:34.520
what you're thinking and what you need,

00:26:34.520 --> 00:26:37.603
you'd like us to give advice on.

00:26:39.740 --> 00:26:41.847
- I can't help but think about my own time

00:26:41.847 --> 00:26:43.660
in graduate school, and particularly

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:45.330
with what Joanne was
saying, where sometimes

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:47.307
you have a problem and you
don't wanna bring it up,

00:26:47.307 --> 00:26:50.760
and the longer you keep
it from the conversation,

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:52.360
the worse it gets, and you just kind of

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:54.880
build this anxiety around it, and I think

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:56.880
that's important what all
of you were saying here

00:26:56.880 --> 00:27:00.840
about just keeping the
constant conversation going.

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:02.270
We're getting a couple
of questions in the chat,

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:03.470
so I'm gonna switch gears a little bit

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:05.930
and sort of encourage
those, because we're here

00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:09.150
to help students think through
the questions that they have.

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:11.500
So the first question
that we received was,

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:13.320
I'd love to hear our
guests reflection on advice

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:15.980
for students who have
co-advisors for a dissertation

00:27:15.980 --> 00:27:19.680
in situations where they
have been a co-advisor,

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:22.240
what has worked best for
you in this situation?

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:24.020
Any tips for students in this situation,

00:27:24.020 --> 00:27:25.440
we have been sort of thinking about

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:27.820
developing a relationship,
but sometimes there are

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:29.640
multiple people in that relationship,

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:31.613
so which adds complication, of course.

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:35.770
- I can lead into that,
because the research

00:27:35.770 --> 00:27:37.540
we do is very collaborative, and in fact,

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:41.150
we've had a collaborative
project with several other

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:43.800
faculty members in the
Chemistry Department,

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:47.610
and so we've had many
formally co-advised students,

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:49.350
and most of them are informally,

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:53.210
but we meet as a team every week, and so

00:27:53.210 --> 00:27:56.830
I think it's important to be consistent

00:27:56.830 --> 00:27:58.650
on what you're saying when, of course,

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:00.260
we may raise questions about

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:03.430
interpretations and
maybe that's a little bit

00:28:03.430 --> 00:28:05.110
different from one advisor to the other,

00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:06.380
but we have to be consistent

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:09.510
in our support and advice on that,

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:10.740
otherwise there could be trouble,

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:14.163
and you need to work together on that,

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:18.853
but I think it could be,

00:28:20.331 --> 00:28:21.560
I think one thing that facilitates that

00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:24.470
is that we meet as a team, and have

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:25.930
round table discussion every week,

00:28:25.930 --> 00:28:28.300
so we can sort of make sure everybody's

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:30.753
working in the same direction.

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:36.390
- So I do a lot of co-advising,

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:39.040
I think probably, I haven't counted,

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:41.140
but maybe more than half
of the dissertations

00:28:41.140 --> 00:28:43.750
I've advised here have been co-advised,

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:46.500
and I think it's really not problematic,

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:47.893
I think it's a great thing.

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:53.270
But we're not working in a team in a lab,

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:57.530
and so usually an advisee
has independent relationships

00:28:57.530 --> 00:28:59.500
with each of the co-advisors and gets

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:02.270
independent feedback from
them, and occasional,

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:04.310
sometimes that's nicely reinforcing,

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.093
we're all saying the same thing,

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:09.990
you need to reorganize in this way

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:12.070
and we both say the same
thing, and that's great,

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:13.930
and sometimes we say different things

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:16.233
and that makes for
interesting conversation.

00:29:17.225 --> 00:29:19.530
And I don't think anybody expects you

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:23.920
to sort of favor one or the other,

00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:25.890
but to follow your own guidance,

00:29:25.890 --> 00:29:28.620
and if there's conflicting
advice to talk it out

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:31.490
and then do what you wanna
do and what you need to do.

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:33.840
So I don't think that it's ever

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:35.350
really been a problematic thing,

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:37.670
and I think it's nice for somebody to have

00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:40.251
more than one person who they feel like is

00:29:40.251 --> 00:29:44.451
their main advisor, so that
there's not just one person

00:29:44.451 --> 00:29:48.787
who is responsible for giving
quick feedback and advice.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:53.567
- Basically every
student that I've advised

00:29:53.567 --> 00:29:55.579
has always been co-advising,

00:29:55.579 --> 00:29:57.410
that's just kind of the structure of

00:29:57.410 --> 00:30:00.473
dissertation committees
within finance and economics.

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:04.820
One problem here is that
oftentimes the advisors

00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:07.330
do ask the students to go
in different directions,

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:10.453
and then the student is
unclear about how to proceed.

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:14.130
I think there it would really be helpful

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:16.780
if the student explained to each advisor

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:23.060
what the other advisors are suggesting,

00:30:23.060 --> 00:30:25.840
because one problem
that I've seen emerge is

00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:29.510
we, and I've heard this
from other faculty members,

00:30:29.510 --> 00:30:32.450
will meet with a student,
recommend some direction,

00:30:32.450 --> 00:30:35.020
a month later the student
hasn't done anything

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:37.620
regarding our suggestion,
and that actually

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:39.870
could be because the other faculty members

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:42.560
are suggesting something else, but it'd be

00:30:42.560 --> 00:30:44.360
really helpful if we understood that

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:46.480
rather than thinking, oh,
it's because you've chosen

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:51.160
to do nothing over the course of a month.

00:30:51.160 --> 00:30:55.190
Another strategy that has
worked is when the student

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:57.410
arranges for an internal presentation,

00:30:57.410 --> 00:31:00.130
all the dissertation members are present,

00:31:00.130 --> 00:31:02.550
and this could be done before kind of

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:04.920
formally scheduled presentation dates,

00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:08.650
and that way all the committee
members are in one room,

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:10.900
and can kind of hash out the differences

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:12.703
in their suggested directions.

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:18.560
- Thank you, there are plenty
of questions coming in now,

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:20.190
so I'm just gonna keep
running through them.

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:22.070
Could you all speak to
the unique challenges

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:23.980
and perhaps opportunities and benefits

00:31:23.980 --> 00:31:26.980
of being a mentor as a
tenure track professor,

00:31:26.980 --> 00:31:29.940
as opposed to a tenured
professor with more job security?

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:32.360
I'm particularly curious
about time management

00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:34.370
and I'd appreciate any
thoughts you have about this.

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:36.440
And this is really important
as students are becoming

00:31:36.440 --> 00:31:38.840
mentors themselves as
well and becoming faculty.

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:48.640
- Yeah, I can maybe jump in on that.

00:31:48.640 --> 00:31:51.240
I think there's a lot of pressure for

00:31:51.240 --> 00:31:54.470
junior faculty members to be productive

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:58.150
and make a name for
themselves and make tenure.

00:31:58.150 --> 00:32:00.750
And sometimes they put undue
pressure on their students,

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:03.820
and I think that's something
that you have to be

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:07.440
careful about, and I think that sometimes

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:10.930
can be leading to very
difficult arrangements.

00:32:10.930 --> 00:32:14.210
And I know in my case it
probably was no exception,

00:32:14.210 --> 00:32:17.060
but I really did try to look into

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:19.420
the direction of the students rather than

00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:22.940
my own individual interests,

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:26.476
but there are realities and demands too,

00:32:26.476 --> 00:32:30.110
and getting the results for
the next grant proposal,

00:32:30.110 --> 00:32:32.880
and we all work as a
team to to try to work

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:34.980
on that and sometimes
that could be stressful,

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:38.460
but I think, again, working
together as opposed to

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:42.383
making demands is a good strategy.

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:51.350
- Within my area of finance
and economics research

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:53.690
I would say there's, I
mean, it's unfortunate,

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:56.870
but there's just no
incentive for junior faculty

00:32:56.870 --> 00:33:00.370
to be effective mentors, or
to devote a lot of time to it.

00:33:00.370 --> 00:33:02.130
Many of them are, but
I think that's because

00:33:02.130 --> 00:33:03.910
they're intrinsically motivated

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:06.220
and they just like research
and talking about that

00:33:06.220 --> 00:33:09.950
with students, but there's
almost nothing in it

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:14.940
for them in terms of
higher tenure probability,

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:16.310
because we don't work in labs,

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:18.350
mentoring students has no direct benefit

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:21.920
to our own research, it's really about,

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:24.067
many people do just like
to engage with students,

00:33:24.067 --> 00:33:26.670
and I think as students
you should try to seek out

00:33:26.670 --> 00:33:30.700
those people, but the way
the institution is designed,

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:34.363
junior faculty have entirely
the wrong set of incentives.

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:38.470
- So it's been a long time since I've been

00:33:38.470 --> 00:33:42.130
a junior faculty member,
but I don't recall it

00:33:42.130 --> 00:33:46.080
being any different in terms
of the kind of mentoring I did.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:50.000
There was a sense in which junior faculty

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:52.900
sometimes can't be a dissertation advisor,

00:33:52.900 --> 00:33:55.470
maybe they can be a co-advisor
in some departments,

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:59.127
but they can't be the
advisor, just because of

00:33:59.127 --> 00:34:02.780
some of the rules we have, and sometimes

00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:06.260
because graduate students
feel closer to people

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:08.430
who are closer to their age and develop

00:34:08.430 --> 00:34:10.230
relationships with them, they sometimes

00:34:10.230 --> 00:34:13.610
lean on junior faculty in ways as if

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:15.920
they're advisors and then that

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:17.930
junior faculty member doesn't get credit

00:34:17.930 --> 00:34:20.610
for being the advisor,
because isn't the advisor,

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:23.480
so I've heard that from
junior faculty members here.

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:26.888
So I think to be careful of,

00:34:26.888 --> 00:34:30.450
to acknowledge that untenured professors

00:34:30.450 --> 00:34:32.560
need to protect their research time

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:34.510
and might not be getting credit for being

00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:37.350
dissertation advisors, even though they're

00:34:37.350 --> 00:34:39.270
offering a kind of mentorship,

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:41.430
so I think that's
something to be aware of,

00:34:41.430 --> 00:34:43.470
but I think that actual mentoring,

00:34:43.470 --> 00:34:44.743
the kinds of mentoring we do,

00:34:44.743 --> 00:34:46.700
I don't think it's all that different

00:34:46.700 --> 00:34:49.150
before you have tenure
and after you have tenure.

00:34:52.510 --> 00:34:54.270
- Thank you, I wanna throw
in a sort of follow up,

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:56.220
which is one of the topics that we had,

00:34:57.140 --> 00:34:59.780
and it's around sort of
building your own mentorship

00:34:59.780 --> 00:35:02.980
as faculty members and how
that's changed over your careers,

00:35:02.980 --> 00:35:05.000
'cause I think that's somewhat
related to this question,

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:07.600
or I'm imagining that this
question is partially about

00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:09.550
students becoming faculty
members and then both

00:35:09.550 --> 00:35:12.120
mentoring graduate students of their own,

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:13.930
but I think it's
important to remember that

00:35:13.930 --> 00:35:16.513
mentorship does not exist
in graduate school alone,

00:35:17.386 --> 00:35:19.410
and that this is something
that will continue to happen.

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:22.330
So does anyone wanna speak to
sort of how you've continued

00:35:22.330 --> 00:35:24.040
to cultivate these sorts of relationships

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:26.360
with colleagues and friends and others?

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:33.040
- Yeah, sure, so I'm still in touch

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:35.370
with my own dissertation advisor

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:38.060
lo these many decades later,

00:35:38.060 --> 00:35:41.410
and so some of these relationships are

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:44.380
lifelong relationships
and that's a good thing.

00:35:44.380 --> 00:35:47.140
And I'm still in touch
with many of my own former

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:51.040
graduate students, again,
lifelong relationships

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:53.040
in the best scenario.

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:58.040
And that's something that
becomes, as Kelly said before,

00:35:58.070 --> 00:36:00.340
more personal over time
is something that might

00:36:00.340 --> 00:36:02.270
start out as a professional relationship,

00:36:02.270 --> 00:36:03.860
becomes more of a friendship and more

00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:06.620
of a peer relationship,
and that's good to know.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:10.260
And I think that all of us will always be

00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:13.240
in need of advice and honest, constructive

00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:17.360
feedback from people, and so the kinds of

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:20.780
work that one does as a mentor or a mentee

00:36:20.780 --> 00:36:22.900
is something that you're always gonna

00:36:22.900 --> 00:36:25.160
want that with other people.

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:28.940
So it's not necessarily
mentoring once you're

00:36:28.940 --> 00:36:31.810
later in life, you don't
necessarily call it mentoring,

00:36:31.810 --> 00:36:33.310
but in some ways it's similar.

00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:38.360
- Yeah, I can certainly echo that.

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:43.295
I've kept in touch with a
lot of my former students,

00:36:43.295 --> 00:36:44.650
in fact, just this semester,

00:36:44.650 --> 00:36:47.440
I had a couple of students who graduated

00:36:47.440 --> 00:36:50.690
more than 30 years ago,
get in touch with me

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:52.930
for advice about career moves that they

00:36:52.930 --> 00:36:57.910
were contemplating, and
that's fairly common,

00:36:57.910 --> 00:37:02.910
that they'll reach out to
ask about this or that.

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:06.250
So I think that's a good thing.

00:37:06.250 --> 00:37:09.160
In fact, lifelong connections are good.

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:11.930
I still keep in touch
with my own PhD advisor

00:37:11.930 --> 00:37:13.650
who's been retired for many years,

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:16.880
but I still talk to him and get insight

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:19.610
from him occasionally, so yeah,

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:24.610
that's definitely important
to maintain connections.

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:30.260
- One thing I'd add is
actually we were talking about

00:37:30.260 --> 00:37:33.500
junior professors, one
piece of advice I have

00:37:33.500 --> 00:37:37.400
is after graduating from PhD
and getting your first job,

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:41.423
especially if that's at
an academics institution,

00:37:41.423 --> 00:37:44.500
it's actually very important
as a junior professor

00:37:44.500 --> 00:37:49.050
to get mentors, and right now I think

00:37:50.060 --> 00:37:53.340
I mentor several junior faculty.

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:55.920
So our business school
recently set up a more formal

00:37:55.920 --> 00:37:59.650
mentorship program between
senior and junior faculty,

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:01.120
that started only two years ago,

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:03.980
and before we didn't
have the system set up,

00:38:03.980 --> 00:38:06.210
so in a place where this
system isn't set up,

00:38:06.210 --> 00:38:09.490
it's really important to go
out and find your own people.

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:12.290
In a PhD program, usually
mentors are assigned to you,

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:15.650
you're in a lab, it's just the
structure is already there,

00:38:15.650 --> 00:38:17.280
whereas once you start your first job,

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:19.430
that's when I think it's really on

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:23.053
the new professors to go out
and find their own network.

00:38:26.783 --> 00:38:27.616
- I'm gonna skip around a little bit,

00:38:27.616 --> 00:38:31.060
because I'm gonna try and
keep these topically related.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:33.270
But so we got a question that's sort of

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:35.671
related to what you were
just speaking to Kelly,

00:38:35.671 --> 00:38:38.180
which is about actually forming
a dissertation committee,

00:38:38.180 --> 00:38:41.020
but how to approach professors
or find the right match,

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:42.950
but also following the comment

00:38:42.950 --> 00:38:46.870
on tenure versus tenure
track, should we try to have

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:48.750
more senior faculty on our committee?

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:50.680
Would you recommend sort of diversifying

00:38:50.680 --> 00:38:53.820
the committee so that you're
getting different voices,

00:38:53.820 --> 00:38:55.740
or I'm reading into
this a little bit more,

00:38:55.740 --> 00:38:57.540
but does anyone wanna speak to that?

00:38:58.960 --> 00:39:01.210
- I didn't mean to imply
that junior faculty

00:39:01.210 --> 00:39:03.540
are not amazing advisors,
I was just commenting

00:39:03.540 --> 00:39:05.980
on the institutional
design, which is often

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:08.180
they don't have incentives to do this,

00:39:08.180 --> 00:39:09.530
although they do it anyway.

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:14.000
In terms of forming a
dissertation committee,

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:16.610
I think it really depends
on the specific people

00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:17.853
that you're considering.

00:39:19.320 --> 00:39:21.330
I think many junior faculty tend to be

00:39:21.330 --> 00:39:23.593
a little bit more detail oriented.

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:26.840
So it actually helps to have
some of the younger people

00:39:26.840 --> 00:39:28.860
on your committee so they can look through

00:39:28.860 --> 00:39:30.300
kind of specific things that you're doing

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:34.490
in your dissertation, your
exact research methods,

00:39:34.490 --> 00:39:36.290
whereas older people might be better

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:40.810
at framing or thinking
about the big picture.

00:39:40.810 --> 00:39:44.318
Now, there are many exceptions
to this generalization,

00:39:44.318 --> 00:39:45.730
but that could be why it's useful to have

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:47.630
all sorts of people on your committee.

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:51.900
- I think it's probably most important

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:56.400
to find the people who you
need and want to work with.

00:39:56.400 --> 00:40:00.083
And often that is a mix
of junior and senior.

00:40:02.010 --> 00:40:04.370
Given Yale's roles I think
you probably need to have

00:40:04.370 --> 00:40:07.350
at least one senior person
on a dissertation committee,

00:40:07.350 --> 00:40:11.080
but I think the most
important thing is finding

00:40:13.097 --> 00:40:15.570
who's gonna help you
accomplish what you wanna do.

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:17.970
And you figure that out as
you're doing your coursework,

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:20.360
sometimes you come to graduate
school kind of knowing

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:22.420
this is the person I wanna work with,

00:40:22.420 --> 00:40:24.730
and sometimes that is
the person you work with

00:40:24.730 --> 00:40:29.160
and sometimes it's not, but
as you do your coursework,

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:31.990
as you study for your comprehensive exams,

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:34.150
as you enter into
writing the dissertation,

00:40:34.150 --> 00:40:37.030
you figure out who are your people,

00:40:37.030 --> 00:40:38.690
and those are the people you ask,

00:40:38.690 --> 00:40:41.320
and I don't think it necessarily matters

00:40:41.320 --> 00:40:45.020
so much about junior and
senior, it matters that

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:48.353
they're the people who are
gonna be able to advise you.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:54.110
- Yeah, I can also say,
I don't think it matters

00:40:54.110 --> 00:40:56.670
so much about junior and
senior, but there are two things

00:40:56.670 --> 00:40:58.950
that I tell students when they're

00:40:58.950 --> 00:41:00.420
trying to decide on their committee.

00:41:00.420 --> 00:41:02.980
One is, those are the people
who will know you best

00:41:02.980 --> 00:41:04.530
when you're graduating,
and they're the ones

00:41:04.530 --> 00:41:06.550
who write letters of
recommendation for you,

00:41:06.550 --> 00:41:10.790
so you need to think about
what they might be able to,

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:12.100
how they might be able to help you

00:41:12.100 --> 00:41:15.220
and advise you in your career path,

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:17.080
but the other thing is you want people

00:41:17.080 --> 00:41:20.920
who know the area well enough
to be able to give advice,

00:41:20.920 --> 00:41:22.200
and so if you wanna say, well,

00:41:22.200 --> 00:41:24.380
I want the most famous
person on my committee,

00:41:24.380 --> 00:41:26.770
because they'll be able to
write the strongest letter,

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:29.010
but if they don't know
the field very well,

00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:30.220
they're not a good committee member,

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:32.290
so we have a discussion

00:41:34.370 --> 00:41:36.270
when a committee is being considered

00:41:37.225 --> 00:41:40.280
and who would you like,
and I would give back

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:42.940
pros and cons, and so we finally

00:41:42.940 --> 00:41:44.820
resolve and come up with a plan for

00:41:44.820 --> 00:41:47.363
who would be the best committee members.

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:54.240
- So there's a question
about how to handle

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:56.480
difficult situations with mentees,

00:41:56.480 --> 00:41:58.390
such as social discord
with other students,

00:41:58.390 --> 00:42:01.040
productivity issues, do
you sometimes need to be

00:42:01.040 --> 00:42:04.240
the bad guy or the bad
person in a situation?

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:06.700
And I think I would tie this back to also

00:42:06.700 --> 00:42:08.440
a topic that came up earlier, which is

00:42:08.440 --> 00:42:10.393
the honesty of feedback.

00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:13.250
And so do you all have sort of...

00:42:14.855 --> 00:42:17.809
I would be open to talking about it as

00:42:17.809 --> 00:42:21.320
needing to do this work
in the best way possible,

00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:24.283
but also if there are difficult
situations that are more,

00:42:27.230 --> 00:42:30.083
take more care to work through?

00:42:36.390 --> 00:42:40.430
- So yeah, there are a range of situations

00:42:40.430 --> 00:42:45.130
and I think it's different
for different students

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:48.340
and the way I see it that

00:42:49.880 --> 00:42:53.340
as I get to know
somebody, I get a sense of

00:42:57.090 --> 00:42:59.430
how much I can push on them in a sense,

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:02.950
that some people are
really open to being pushed

00:43:02.950 --> 00:43:05.750
and want me to push them, and some people

00:43:07.064 --> 00:43:10.393
are maybe more sensitive to being pushed,

00:43:11.500 --> 00:43:14.653
and I take more care with my honest,

00:43:15.677 --> 00:43:17.900
I'm honest with them
all and I push them all,

00:43:17.900 --> 00:43:20.490
but some people I can be
a little more blunt with

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:23.830
than with others, and I
think that's a matter of

00:43:23.830 --> 00:43:26.970
the same when in friendships
and with real relatives,

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:29.950
you figure out different people
have different personalities

00:43:29.950 --> 00:43:32.190
and you try to learn to work with them

00:43:33.574 --> 00:43:36.060
to be supportive of them in
a way that'll work for them.

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:38.280
And so it's not always the same,

00:43:38.280 --> 00:43:40.350
but there are difficult conversations

00:43:40.350 --> 00:43:42.960
and sometimes you have to
have difficult conversations

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:46.560
and you have to, it's
not necessarily pleasant,

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:50.120
but you might need to be involved

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:52.453
in a difficult conversation,
and that's okay.

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:02.100
- I guess my thought on this is that

00:44:02.100 --> 00:44:04.780
the best way to handle this is to avoid it

00:44:04.780 --> 00:44:08.070
in the first place, so
foster good relationships

00:44:08.070 --> 00:44:12.130
from the very start, and I
think that can oftentimes

00:44:12.130 --> 00:44:14.510
build the sense of
teamwork and collaboration

00:44:14.510 --> 00:44:17.270
that will make it less
likely that that will happen,

00:44:17.270 --> 00:44:19.120
but I think as Joanne said, sometimes

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:23.310
situations come up and
best way to approach that

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:26.000
is just to be honest and face up,

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:28.293
not to let it fester and get worse.

00:44:30.510 --> 00:44:32.920
- For some of my students who have had,

00:44:32.920 --> 00:44:35.310
the issues can range a full spectrum,

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:38.310
but what has been helpful
in the past is actually

00:44:38.310 --> 00:44:40.993
I've suggested a leave of absence,

00:44:42.231 --> 00:44:43.870
and that's been helpful
just to take a break

00:44:43.870 --> 00:44:46.393
from the university environment.

00:44:47.260 --> 00:44:51.100
It's actually viewed perhaps better

00:44:51.100 --> 00:44:54.800
to have a leave of absence when
you're searching for a job.

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:56.990
It's kind of assumed that
it was for personal reasons

00:44:56.990 --> 00:44:59.320
rather than to have
stayed in a PhD program

00:44:59.320 --> 00:45:01.640
for a very long time
without a leave of absence

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:04.220
as you're sorting through the same issues,

00:45:04.220 --> 00:45:06.320
so I found that could be quite helpful.

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:10.280
Other than that, I don't view it as my job

00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:12.670
to be a parent or to dress down a student,

00:45:12.670 --> 00:45:15.390
so if some student is
really just not working,

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:18.810
working harder or having some kind of

00:45:18.810 --> 00:45:21.320
misbehavior issues, I mean,
there's going to be a limit,

00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:23.680
I might comment on it
once, but I'm not going to

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:26.870
further dress down a student.

00:45:26.870 --> 00:45:28.270
I don't view that as my job.

00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:31.560
- Thank you for that.

00:45:35.350 --> 00:45:37.840
So there's a question about how could we

00:45:37.840 --> 00:45:41.733
systematically reward good
mentorship at this institution?

00:45:43.699 --> 00:45:44.960
So we started talking
about this a little bit,

00:45:44.960 --> 00:45:47.490
Kelly was sort of mentioning this,

00:45:47.490 --> 00:45:48.810
so how could we do it?

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:53.410
And I would also say, what
is rewarding about it?

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:56.130
As three people who have
received this award,

00:45:56.130 --> 00:45:59.123
there must be some incentive
for you personally.

00:46:01.990 --> 00:46:04.100
- Well, I can say the
biggest measure of success

00:46:04.100 --> 00:46:05.903
I have is how successful the students

00:46:05.903 --> 00:46:09.390
that have worked with me go
on after they've graduated,

00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:11.420
and I think that's your legacy,

00:46:11.420 --> 00:46:14.290
so that's where I would see my reward,

00:46:14.290 --> 00:46:17.223
is the success of the
students that worked with me.

00:46:24.200 --> 00:46:26.840
- Yeah, I agree, it's not really about

00:46:26.840 --> 00:46:28.753
direct rewards from the university,

00:46:30.835 --> 00:46:33.650
it's very fun and interesting
to talk to students

00:46:33.650 --> 00:46:37.063
about their research ideas and
to guide that process along,

00:46:39.060 --> 00:46:41.890
and I like to see how
their projects develop

00:46:41.890 --> 00:46:43.810
and where their careers lead,

00:46:43.810 --> 00:46:45.483
so that's the reward.

00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:49.700
- Yeah, I mean, I agree with what

00:46:49.700 --> 00:46:53.451
both Gary and Kelly said,
and I would also say

00:46:53.451 --> 00:46:56.080
that I learn a lot from
my graduate students.

00:46:56.080 --> 00:47:00.140
They work on projects that
it's not this lab project

00:47:00.140 --> 00:47:01.930
where we're all working on the same thing,

00:47:01.930 --> 00:47:04.570
they work independently
on their own projects,

00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:07.233
and I've learned so much
from reading their work,

00:47:08.300 --> 00:47:11.394
and I get to help shape a work

00:47:11.394 --> 00:47:14.115
and I don't have to do all the research.

00:47:14.115 --> 00:47:16.410
So I love sort of reading
their work and helping

00:47:16.410 --> 00:47:19.150
their work along and I learn from that.

00:47:19.150 --> 00:47:21.170
And that's part of the reward for me,

00:47:21.170 --> 00:47:23.820
the relationships, the
lifelong relationships,

00:47:23.820 --> 00:47:27.230
that's important to me,
that's a reward for sure.

00:47:27.230 --> 00:47:30.640
And then there is the award
that we all three got,

00:47:30.640 --> 00:47:33.810
that was nice too, so there are ways

00:47:33.810 --> 00:47:35.460
that this is rewarding, for sure.

00:47:38.000 --> 00:47:40.000
- So we sort of answered
parts of a question

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:42.610
that just came in, but
there's a separate piece

00:47:42.610 --> 00:47:44.150
that builds on what we
were just talking about,

00:47:44.150 --> 00:47:45.640
which is how could we facilitate

00:47:45.640 --> 00:47:47.740
improving mentorship skills?

00:47:47.740 --> 00:47:50.360
So I guess my pre-question to that is

00:47:50.360 --> 00:47:53.960
what do you think the key skills
to being a good mentor are?

00:47:53.960 --> 00:47:56.430
And then how would you
encourage graduate students

00:47:56.430 --> 00:47:58.470
to build those skills or faculty members

00:47:58.470 --> 00:48:00.347
to build those skills?

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:08.120
- One of the things I
encourage graduate students

00:48:08.120 --> 00:48:09.640
and postdocs who've working with me

00:48:09.640 --> 00:48:12.270
is to mentor undergraduates and actually

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:15.150
have an experience in
mentorship while they're here

00:48:15.150 --> 00:48:19.370
and we can work as a team to

00:48:19.370 --> 00:48:23.080
learn how to do that
effectively and gain experience.

00:48:23.080 --> 00:48:25.970
I think once you go off to
become a faculty member,

00:48:25.970 --> 00:48:27.590
it's not cold, you just
have to start learning

00:48:27.590 --> 00:48:30.403
how to be a mentor at
that point, you can build

00:48:30.403 --> 00:48:32.973
your knowledge as you're
going through school.

00:48:38.690 --> 00:48:41.360
- I guess I would say, I would add to that

00:48:41.360 --> 00:48:44.540
to be open to these
kinds of relationships,

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:47.900
to listen, to listen is really important,

00:48:47.900 --> 00:48:49.163
to be honest,

00:48:53.690 --> 00:48:55.250
to be welcoming.

00:48:55.250 --> 00:48:58.410
Those seem to me things that are important

00:48:58.410 --> 00:49:00.990
just sort of in general
and kind of in life,

00:49:00.990 --> 00:49:03.440
but I think they work in
mentoring relationships.

00:49:06.100 --> 00:49:09.023
- I never got any training
in how to be a mentor,

00:49:10.660 --> 00:49:13.150
it wasn't part of the job search process,

00:49:13.150 --> 00:49:16.270
or part of the PhD program, and I imagine

00:49:16.270 --> 00:49:19.000
most of my colleagues
also didn't get training

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:22.790
in how to be a mentor,
so here's where I think

00:49:22.790 --> 00:49:26.960
having talking to multiple faculty members

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:29.150
can help, we're all
imperfect and we don't have

00:49:29.150 --> 00:49:32.410
formal training, so it's
useful for students,

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:34.480
I think, then to piece
together what they need

00:49:34.480 --> 00:49:36.793
across multiple different faculty members.

00:49:40.580 --> 00:49:42.160
- So we're coming on
time and I know we have

00:49:42.160 --> 00:49:44.373
a hard stop at one, but
I wanted to offer you all

00:49:44.373 --> 00:49:47.910
the chance to give any
last pieces of advice

00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:50.530
as graduate students are
thinking through their own

00:49:50.530 --> 00:49:52.880
mentorship philosophies or other things

00:49:52.880 --> 00:49:54.443
that you wanted to say
during this conversation

00:49:54.443 --> 00:49:56.593
that we just haven't
been able to touch on.

00:50:00.620 --> 00:50:03.070
- Well, I guess I would say that

00:50:03.070 --> 00:50:06.510
you are great observers and
you can observe your own

00:50:06.510 --> 00:50:10.100
professors now and your own
mentors and advisors now,

00:50:10.100 --> 00:50:13.970
and that'll help you see what
kind of mentor you wanna be.

00:50:13.970 --> 00:50:16.490
You will see, you will have people who

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:19.990
have completely different
personalities than you do,

00:50:19.990 --> 00:50:21.380
and maybe their mentoring style

00:50:21.380 --> 00:50:22.840
wouldn't be your mentoring style,

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:25.410
but you will find people who
are role models of sorts,

00:50:25.410 --> 00:50:28.730
I hope, and you'll be able
to pick up things from them

00:50:28.730 --> 00:50:31.970
and learn how how you
might pay it forward.

00:50:31.970 --> 00:50:34.823
I know I learned a lot from
my own dissertation advisor,

00:50:34.823 --> 00:50:38.800
I just celebrated her
retirement last month

00:50:38.800 --> 00:50:42.170
out in California, and I know
I learned a lot from her,

00:50:42.170 --> 00:50:43.760
but she's a different personality,

00:50:43.760 --> 00:50:48.220
and I had to figure out what
is my own style of mentoring,

00:50:48.220 --> 00:50:50.860
and I did that partly by
observing other people

00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:53.853
and partly by knowing myself
and knowing what works for me.

00:50:57.855 --> 00:51:00.260
- Yeah, I can say that, I
mean, I agree with that,

00:51:00.260 --> 00:51:03.360
that you learn by observing, in my case,

00:51:03.360 --> 00:51:06.040
when I started out there, there
were no mentoring programs,

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:09.010
we never had any formal mentorship,

00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:12.380
I don't think that was even
talked about in those days,

00:51:12.380 --> 00:51:14.050
so that's now that's been put in place,

00:51:14.050 --> 00:51:16.397
which is a great thing, but
that's not all there is,

00:51:16.397 --> 00:51:20.700
you can reach out to people
who can provide advice,

00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:23.140
there's both formal
and informal mentoring,

00:51:23.140 --> 00:51:25.534
and in fact, informal
mentoring might even be

00:51:25.534 --> 00:51:29.080
in many cases the most
useful for career directions

00:51:29.080 --> 00:51:31.990
if you find somebody who has a career path

00:51:31.990 --> 00:51:35.243
or ideas that help best fit with yours,

00:51:36.330 --> 00:51:38.800
reach out and those kinds of

00:51:38.800 --> 00:51:41.023
informal mentoring can be really useful.

00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:45.210
- I'd just like to echo
something that I think

00:51:45.210 --> 00:51:49.100
Joanne said about checking
in often with your mentors.

00:51:49.100 --> 00:51:50.700
I think this can be very useful,

00:51:50.700 --> 00:51:52.810
especially because
students in the beginning

00:51:52.810 --> 00:51:56.320
are concerned I think about
making a good impression,

00:51:56.320 --> 00:51:58.780
and that leads to stress
that actually leads them

00:51:58.780 --> 00:52:00.870
to just not meet up with us at all,

00:52:00.870 --> 00:52:03.330
until they have made significant progress

00:52:03.330 --> 00:52:04.900
on their dissertation.

00:52:04.900 --> 00:52:07.900
And I think that's unfortunate,
because that can lead

00:52:07.900 --> 00:52:12.030
to a lot of wasted effort
in various directions,

00:52:12.030 --> 00:52:15.230
and actually by checking
in with us frequently,

00:52:15.230 --> 00:52:18.153
that actually allows us
to update our beliefs,

00:52:19.040 --> 00:52:22.630
so one meeting in which you
didn't have your work done

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:25.610
is absolutely fine, as
long as you check with us

00:52:25.610 --> 00:52:27.880
regularly in future months
and throughout the year

00:52:27.880 --> 00:52:30.030
and we can see the
progress of the project.

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:35.960
- Great, well, I wanna thank you all

00:52:35.960 --> 00:52:39.730
for your incredibly wise
and judicious advice today

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:42.843
and just for being so gracious
with your time and insights.

00:52:44.163 --> 00:52:46.870
And I also want to thank
everyone who participated

00:52:46.870 --> 00:52:50.500
in the conversation with the
questions through the chat.

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:52.900
I should say that we do intend to post

00:52:52.900 --> 00:52:55.350
the recording of the
conversation on YouTube,

00:52:55.350 --> 00:52:57.693
so we'll get that link out to folks soon.

00:52:59.110 --> 00:53:00.600
And yes, thank you all for being here,

00:53:00.600 --> 00:53:02.690
we really appreciate
it, and we look forward

00:53:02.690 --> 00:53:03.940
to seeing you again soon.

